It is useful to put the equations into two columns before doing anything with the wave. In empty space they are $\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot\mathbf B&=0,
&
\dot{\mathbf B}&=\nabla\times\mathbf E,

\\
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E&=0,
&
\dot{\mathbf E}&=-\,\nabla\times\mathbf B.
\end{aligned}$ We use the mostly-plus metric $\eta_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the course convention $F_{\mu\nu}
=
\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu,
\qquad
F_{0i}=E_i,
\qquad
F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k.$ Then the same two families can be written $\begin{aligned}
\partial_\mu F_{\nu\sigma}
+\partial_\nu F_{\sigma\mu}
+\partial_\sigma F_{\mu\nu}
&=0,

\\
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}
&=0.
\end{aligned}$

