p.src:
The conventional normalization is $\boxed{
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{EM}}
=
-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
}$ or $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{EM}}
=
\frac12(E^2-B^2).$ The minus sign gives the electric term the familiar positive sign. The factor $1/4$ compensates the twofold appearance of each antisymmetric pair and leaves the conventional factor $1/2$. Multiplying the entire free action by a nonzero constant would not change its source-free equations, but this normalization is the one that fits the standard source coupling.

